  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A widespread, soaking rainfall along with windy \nconditions are expected for Thanksgiving Day, with unsettled weather \nlasting into the weekend.\n\nNorthern and southern stream energy will phase with a large \nupper level low taking shape over the Tennessee Valley, as broad \nsurface low pressure tracks north into the Carolinas today, \nwith a trough extending westward into the lower Ohio Valley. The\nsystem is then forecast to continue northward into the Northeast\ntonight into Friday. Widespread precipitation will spread \nnorthward into the region on today as plume of deep moisture \ntransport develops within an area of strong deformation along \nthe north flank of the upper low. \n\nDry conditions expected this morning before precipitation expands \nnorthward into the western Southern Tier by late morning. With \na deep layer of low level dry air in place, initial precipitation\ntype could be a mix of rain and wet snowflakes due to wet bulbing,\nfollowed by a transition to all rain as the warm nose strengthens.\nThe precipitation will then spread slowly northward through the\nremainder of the day into early evening. The North Country may \neven remain rain free well into the afternoon. Strong easterly \njet aloft and tight surface pressure gradient will bring windy \nconditions this afternoon through at least the first half of \ntonight. Winds will likely remain just below below advisory \ncriteria, gusting to 35 to 45 mph, with some isolated gusts 45+ \nmph possible over the lakeshore of Niagara and Orleans counties,\nand farther south downwind of the Chautauqua ridge.\n\nTemperatures aloft remain quite mild with profiles suggesting almost \nall rain as the primary precipitation type through Friday morning. \nA brief period of sleet is possible in higher elevations due to\nwet-bulbing, but this will quickly change to plain rain as\nprecipitation continues.\n\nFriday through Saturday, occasional showers will continue as the \ndeep mid level closed low crosses the region. The airmass will grow \ncold enough for some lake enhancement by Friday and Saturday, with\nindications pointing towards westerly flow during this time frame,\ngradually becoming southwesterly late Saturday into Saturday \nnight. Late Friday through Saturday, modest cold advection will \ndrop 850 MB temperatures down to -2C to -3C. Latest sounding \ndata and thickness levels suggest rain for lower elevations, but\nsome wet snow may start to mix in across higher terrain. Marginal\ntemperatures at the surface and aloft suggest there will be \nlittle, if any snow accumulation during this period, even across\nhigher terrain. Best chance for a light coating would be across\nthe higher terrain of the western Southern Tier.\n\nRainfall totals with this system remain in the 1.0\" to 1.5\" \nrange, but with much of the area in D1 moderate drought after \nthe exceptionally dry conditions since September, this is \nexpected to be a beneficial rainfall with minimal hydro concerns\nat this time.",
